// ── BIOME EXPLORER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const biomeData = {
  rainforest: { title:'Tropical Rainforest', body:'The highest biodiversity biome on Earth. Amazon basin: ~10% of all species on 0.5% of Earth\'s surface. Rainfall: >2,000 mm/year. Temperature: stable 25–30°C year-round. Structured into five layers: emergent (60m+), canopy (30–45m), understory, shrub layer, and forest floor. Nutrient paradox: soils are poor (nutrients leach rapidly in high rainfall), but ecosystems are hyperproductive because nutrients are almost entirely locked in living biomass. Mycorrhizal networks transfer nutrients directly between organisms. Counterintuitive fact: 80% of Amazon nutrients come from Saharan dust blown across the Atlantic. Deforestation threat: 17% of Amazon already deforested; models suggest 20–25% as a tipping point toward savannification.' },
  savanna: { title:'Tropical Savanna', body:'The African savanna supports the largest remaining megafauna on Earth: wildebeest (1.3 million), zebra (~700,000), elephant (~400,000 in Africa), lion, hyena, giraffe. Defined by mixture of grasses and widely spaced trees; maintained by the interaction of fire, drought, and grazing. Fire is not a disturbance but an integral ecosystem process: many savanna plants have fire-adapted traits (thick bark, underground storage organs). The Serengeti-Mara ecosystem supports the largest remaining land migration on Earth: 1.3 million wildebeest following seasonal rains in a circular 800 km annual circuit. This migration fertilises the landscape with ~22,000 tonnes of dung per day.' },
  desert: { title:'Desert', body:'Deserts cover ~33% of Earth\'s land surface and are defined by aridity (<250 mm precipitation/year), not necessarily heat. The Sahara is hot; the Gobi is cold; Antarctica is the world\'s largest desert. Biodiversity is adapted, not absent: Namib Desert fog beetles harvest water from coastal fog. Saharan silver ants (Cataglyphis bombycina) forage at surface temperatures of 60°C — the highest temperature any animal is known to survive. Biological soil crusts (cyanobacteria, lichen, moss) in cold deserts fix nitrogen and prevent erosion; they take 50–250 years to form and are destroyed by a single footstep. Desert expansion (desertification) affects 40% of Earth\'s dryland areas and threatens 250 million people.' },
  mediterranean: { title:'Mediterranean Shrublands', body:'Found in five regions: Mediterranean Basin, California, central Chile, South African Cape, and SW Australia — all at 30–40° latitude, west coasts of continents. Characterised by dry summers, mild wet winters, and fire-adapted vegetation (chaparral, maquis, fynbos, kwongan). Fire is so integral that many plants require it to germinate: South Africa\'s fynbos (which contains ~9,600 plant species in an area the size of Portugal — more than the entire Amazon per unit area) has seeds that only open with smoke exposure. Mediterranean climates are threatened by climate change shifting the moisture regimes; the Mediterranean Basin may see 40% less precipitation under 3°C warming.' },
  temperate: { title:'Temperate Forest', body:'Deciduous (broad-leaved) forests dominate eastern North America, Europe, and East Asia. Evergreen temperate forests in Pacific Northwest (Douglas fir, Sitka spruce — some trees >1,000 years old) store more carbon per hectare than tropical rainforests. Seasonal leaf drop is an adaptation to cold winters; it allows trees to avoid freeze damage while losing minimal water. Temperate forests have been heavily deforested for agriculture over millennia: Europe has lost ~45% of its original forest, mostly before 1800. They are now recovering in many regions — Europe\'s forest cover is actually increasing. The forest understory is ecologically critical: 80% of woodland species live in the lower 2 metres.' },
  boreal: { title:'Boreal Forest (Taiga)', body:'The world\'s largest terrestrial biome: 14 million km² across Russia, Canada, and Scandinavia. Dominated by conifers (spruce, fir, larch, pine) adapted to short growing seasons and nutrient-poor, cold soils. Holds ~30–40% of all terrestrial carbon, much of it in soils and permafrost. The permafrost question is existential: ~25% of the Northern Hemisphere has permafrost, storing an estimated 1.5 trillion tonnes of carbon. If permafrost thaws rapidly (as is occurring 70+ years ahead of IPCC projections in Siberia), it will release enormous quantities of CO2 and methane — a positive feedback that could push Earth past 2°C regardless of human emission cuts. Boreal forests burn extensively every year; Russian boreal fires in 2021 released more CO2 than many entire countries.' },
  tundra: { title:'Tundra', body:'The treeless zone above the boreal forest, characterised by permafrost, extreme cold, and a very short growing season (6–10 weeks). Arctic tundra is warming at 3–4 times the global average rate — the "Arctic amplification" effect driven by ice-albedo feedback. Species include lemmings (whose 3–4 year population cycles drive predator dynamics), Arctic foxes, musk oxen, reindeer/caribou. Tundra contains extensive peat deposits; as it thaws, it transitions from carbon sink to carbon source. The treeline is advancing northward at ~30–60 km per decade, converting tundra to boreal forest — which is darker and absorbs more heat, another positive feedback.' },
  wetlands: { title:'Wetlands', body:'The most threatened and most carbon-dense ecosystem type. Mangroves (tropical coastal wetlands) store up to 1,000 tC/ha in their soils — 3–5 times more carbon per hectare than tropical rainforests. They also protect 18 million km of coastline, support ~80% of tropical fishery species at some life stage, and provide natural flood buffering worth hundreds of billions of dollars annually. Yet 50% of the world\'s mangroves have been destroyed since 1950, primarily for shrimp aquaculture. Peatlands (see Part M discussion) cover 3% of land but hold ~550–650 GtC. Constructed wetlands can treat wastewater effectively, and wetland restoration provides multiple co-benefits: carbon, biodiversity, water filtration, flood protection.' },
  freshwater: { title:'Freshwater Ecosystems', body:'Rivers, lakes, streams, ponds, and aquifers. Freshwater covers <1% of Earth\'s surface but supports ~10% of all species — disproportionately high biodiversity. It is also the most threatened ecosystem type: freshwater species have declined 83% since 1970 (Living Planet Index), compared to 69% for vertebrates overall. Primary threats: dams (over 58,000 large dams worldwide fragment river systems), water extraction (many rivers no longer reach the sea — the Colorado River, Yellow River), pollution, invasive species. Amazon river dolphins, giant otters, freshwater stingrays — charismatic species critically threatened. Underground aquifer systems (the Ogallala, the Arabian aquifer system) are being drawn down at rates far exceeding recharge.' },
  marine: { title:'Marine Ecosystems', body:'Oceans cover 71% of Earth\'s surface and contain 97% of Earth\'s water. The epipelagic zone (sunlit top 200m) drives most marine productivity. Open ocean is the "marine desert" — vast, deep, and nutrient-poor. Productivity concentrates in coastal upwelling zones (Humboldt Current, Benguela Current) where deep nutrient-rich water rises to the surface. These zones cover <1% of ocean area but provide ~20% of global fish catch. Ocean absorbs ~26% of annual CO2 emissions and ~90% of excess heat from global warming. Ocean deoxygenation (oxygen-minimum zones expanding by ~3–8% per decade) threatens deep-sea ecosystems and fisheries. The deep sea is the last large biome on Earth barely explored but increasingly targeted for mining.' },
  coral: { title:'Coral Reefs', body:'Called the "rainforests of the sea": covering 0.1–0.5% of ocean floor but supporting ~25% of all marine species at some life stage. Built by tiny coral polyps secreting calcium carbonate skeletons; requires clear, warm (18–30°C), shallow, oligotrophic water. Symbiotic zooxanthellae algae live within coral tissue, providing up to 90% of the coral\'s energy via photosynthesis — when temperatures exceed ~1°C above seasonal maximum for 8+ weeks, zooxanthellae are expelled (bleaching), and coral starves. Mass bleaching events were unknown before 1998; the Great Barrier Reef has experienced 5 mass bleaching events since 2016. Current trajectory: 70–90% of coral reefs will functionally collapse at 1.5°C; virtually all at 2°C.' },
  polar: { title:'Polar Ecosystems', body:'Arctic and Antarctic ecosystems are among the most rapidly changing on Earth. Arctic sea ice summer extent has declined ~40% since 1979. The Arctic is effectively ice-free in summer by ~2040 under current trajectories. Antarctic Peninsula has warmed ~3°C since 1950 — faster than any other region on Earth except parts of the Arctic. Polar food webs are simple and highly dependent on key species: Antarctic krill (Euphausia superba) form the base of nearly every Southern Ocean food chain, supporting penguins, seals, whales, and seabirds. Krill populations have declined ~80% in regions where sea ice has retreated. Polar bears (Arctic) are thermally stressed, losing sea ice hunting platforms. The cryosphere\'s collapse affects global sea level, ocean circulation, and climate via feedback loops that amplify warming far beyond the poles.' }
};

